# Deterministic bytes for the things whose bytes are the evidence.
#
# Stage 0 R6, 2026-09-10. `docs/research/2026-09-10_validation_audit.md`
# finding 7: preflight step 5 compares NORMALISED LF bytes against the version
# archive, which is the right check for semantic identity and says nothing
# about the exact sha256 that reaches AIcrowd. The submitted artifact is
# hashed at submit time, so nothing published is wrong -- but a repository
# whose working tree holds three different line-ending conventions across its
# Python files cannot make "the exact bytes" mean one thing.
#
# The index was ALREADY all-LF (`git ls-files --eol`: 206 w/lf, 51 w/crlf, one
# w/mixed, every one of them i/lf), so this changes no committed byte. It
# fixes the working tree, which is where a candidate file is read from.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf

# PowerShell is run by PowerShell, on Windows, and CRLF is its native form.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are executed by bash, including inside WSL, where a CR is a
# syntax error rather than whitespace.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Frozen evidence. Never normalised, never diffed: these are bytes, not text.
experiments/2026-08-21_phase2_manifest/cache/*.raw -text -diff
*.npz -text -diff

# THE VAULT SEAL IS ALREADY LF IN THE INDEX, AND STAYS THAT WAY.
# The first draft of this file marked `experiments/vault/*` as `-text`, meaning
# "store the working tree verbatim". The working tree held CRLF, so git
# immediately staged three seal artifacts -- README.md, manifest.json and
# touch_log.jsonl -- with different bytes. The seal's whole job is that its
# bytes do not move. Caught before it was committed; recorded here so nobody
# reintroduces it as a tidy-up.
experiments/vault/*.md text eol=lf
experiments/vault/*.json text eol=lf
experiments/vault/*.jsonl text eol=lf

